From 42d5c5cb6dca39f6926c9d271119f57c758ddcd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ae86zhizhi <550149470@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 14:56:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(scan): keep chunk boundaries out of pretokens that
 trail past a newline

The punctuation-run alternative of the recognized patterns ends with a
trailing class after its [^\s\p{L}\p{N}]+ run: [\r\n]* for Kimi and
[\r\n/]* for o200k. scan_core consumed that class when deciding where a
pretoken ends, but newline_chunk_bounds placed the chunk boundary straight
after the last newline of the surrounding whitespace run. For o200k the two
disagree whenever a newline is followed by '/', so a pretoken such as
'@@\n//' was cut across two chunks and both halves got different ids --
silently, with no length change to notice, and only on inputs long enough
to be chunked.

The two were the same rule written twice, so they are now one:
newline_tail_byte(kind, byte) is shared by scan_core and the chunker.
newline_chunk_bounds asks whether the newline sits inside a punctuation run
-- by walking back over \r/\n only, never over the rest of the trailing
class, which would run into the punctuation run itself and misread it --
and if so ends the chunk at the end of the whole trailing class.

Tests: chunked scanning must equal whole-buffer scanning for both kinds
across 13 needle shapes, 3 input sizes, 5 placements and 7 chunk counts;
the trailing class is pinned against the pattern constants.
